This document specifies a Hindley-Milner style type inference system extended with refinement types and linear types. The goal is to provide static type checking with a minimal annotation burden on the user, suitable for the CSLv3 reference compiler.

We define a positive-float type, which is a 32-bit floating point value constrained to be greater than zero. We define a unit-vector type, which is a 3-dimensional vector constrained to have magnitude approximately equal to one. We define a bounded-health type, which is a 16-bit unsigned integer whose maximum value is bounded by an externally supplied constant.

The make-vector operation takes an unsigned 32-bit length and returns a 3-dimensional vector whose length depends on that input value, giving it a Pi-type signature. The pair-len-and-data operation takes an unsigned 32-bit length and returns a dependent pair of the length value together with the vector itself, a Sigma-type signature. The normalize operation takes a mutable reference to a 3-dimensional vector and returns a boolean success indicator, operating on an existing buffer rather than allocating. The borrow-shared operation takes a shared reference to a string and returns a boolean, where the reference is read-only and may be shared with other callers. The borrow-mut operation takes an exclusive mutable reference to a string and returns a boolean, where the reference grants write access and may not be aliased.

The refinement soundness invariant states that any runtime value inhabiting a refinement type must actually satisfy the attached predicate at runtime. Linear variables must be used exactly once, not zero times and not more than one time, and any deviation is a type error caught at compile time.

The tests assert that the identity function has type a-to-a, that adding one has type i32-to-i32, and that function application has type (a-to-b)-to-a-to-b. Self-application fails the occurs check because it would require a type that contains itself.
